     "type": "concept",
     "title": "The Quest for Quantum Gravity",
     "content": "If neither theory alone can handle a singularity, physics needs a deeper theory that contains **both** — one that becomes ordinary relativity for big things and ordinary quantum mechanics for small things, but stays sensible even where they overlap. This holy grail has a name: **quantum gravity**. Nobody has finished it, but there are serious candidates.\n\n**String theory.** Its bold idea: the fundamental objects are not point particles but tiny vibrating **strings**, and different vibrations look like different particles, much as different notes come from one violin string. String theory naturally includes a quantum version of gravity, but it requires extra hidden dimensions of space and, so far, makes few predictions we can test.\n\n**Loop quantum gravity.** Its idea: **spacetime itself is not smooth**. Zoom in far enough and it is woven from tiny discrete loops, a finest possible grain, so you cannot keep dividing forever. This dissolves the infinities by forbidding a true zero-size point.\n\nThere is also a sharp puzzle pointing the way, the **black-hole information paradox**: quantum mechanics insists information can never be destroyed, yet things that fall into a black hole seem to vanish forever when it slowly evaporates. Resolving that clash is widely seen as a crucial clue. **All of this is open, active, unfinished research.** You are looking at the true frontier.",
